<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Rwagasore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1932">1932</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Prince Louis Rwagasore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Prince Louis Rwagasore was a Burundian nationalist, son of Mwami <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwambutsa-iv">Mwambutsa IV</a>, who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-for-national-progress">UPRONA</a> party to victory in the elections of September 1961. He became prime minister but was assassinated in October 1961 by a Greek gunman hired by politicians of a rival party. He is honoured as Burundi's national hero of independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-rwagasore.jpg" alt="Louis Rwagasore" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Postal administration of the Kingdom of Burundi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BDI_1963_MiNr0044A_pm_B002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rwagasore was born on 10 January 1932 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a>, Ruanda-Urundi, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwambutsa-iv">Mwambutsa IV</a> and Thérèse Kanyonga, into the Bezi clan of the Ganwa, an aristocratic group often associated with the Tutsis. He attended Catholic schools and the Groupe Scolaire d'Astrida, then went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1951 and earned a degree in political economy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-university-of-leuven">Catholic University of Leuven</a>. He returned in December 1956. On 12 September 1959 he married a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> woman, Marie-Rose Ntamikevyo; their two daughters died in infancy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In June 1957 he founded the Traders' Cooperatives of Burundi to let native Urundians control their own commerce, building support among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> traders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Usumbura</a>; the Belgian administration took the venture over, and his national profile grew. As leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-for-national-progress">Union for National Progress</a> he sought independence, national unity and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, with a leadership balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutus</a> and Tutsis. On 27 October 1960, before the municipal elections, the administration put him under house arrest, hoping to weaken his party, which then boycotted the vote. He was released on 9 December, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> scrutiny restored his political rights. He became prime minister on 28 September 1961.</p><p>On 13 October 1961 he was shot in the throat while dining at the Hotel Tanganyika in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Usumbura</a>. Police arrested the gunman, Ioannis Kageorgis, and three members of the rival PDC, and investigators concluded that the PDC leaders Jean-Baptiste Ntidendereza and Joseph Biroli, distant cousins of his, had planned the killing, probably with the support of the Belgian Resident. Kageorgis was executed on 30 June 1962, and five others were hanged on 15 January 1963 after a retrial.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His death derailed his efforts to build inter-ethnic cohesion, fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a>–Tutsi tensions and fractured his party, which later used his image while becoming a vehicle for Tutsi-elitist single-party rule. A mausoleum was built at his grave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a>, and 13 October became a public holiday. Within Burundi he enjoys nearly universal acclaim, though the historian Christine Deslaurier writes that the consensus rests more on his martyrdom than on his political thought.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Louis Rwagasore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Prince Louis Rwagasore</b>, eldest son of Mwambutsa IV, who led the nationalist party UPRONA to victory in 1961, is honoured as the hero of independence, and the anniversary of his death, 13 October, is a public holiday.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-burundi">Kingdom of Burundi · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Power rested with a Tutsi-dominated army and the single party UPRONA, founded by the independence hero <b>Prince Louis Rwagasore</b>, who had been assassinated in 1961.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-burundi">Republic of Burundi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujumbura">Bujumbura</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-3.43,29.93&amp;year=1950" aria-label="Gitega on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5381 2292 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis Rwagasore"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5831 2562h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5831 2562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-3.43,29.93&amp;year=1950">What was here in 1950? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
